      "text": "The run looks **ANOMALOUS**: exhaust oxygen has risen steadily through growth, and the most likely cause is air leaking in through a seal. I've proposed pausing the run.\n\n- **Oxygen:** it sat near 5 ppm early in growth (t=2640 s) after the purge, then rose to 13, 19, 27 and 33 ppm by t=3120 s.\n- **Why a seal leak:** argon flow is steady at 100 sccm and pressure is flat at about 761 Torr, so the gas controller and a blocked exhaust are both unlikely. Because the tube is at atmospheric pressure, air can get in without the pressure reading changing.\n- **Temperature:** holding at 750 °C with normal heater power.\n\nThere's no normal-run reference for oxygen at this stage, so I can't say how far outside the usual range 33 ppm is. A leak check or a pressure-decay test would confirm the seal. There's no sample characterization yet, so nothing can be said about growth. And because oxygen got in during growth, any result from this run may not tell you much about the recipe itself."
     }
